# Cursor Rules — Vercel Deployment

Prototypes deploy to Vercel using the Vercel CLI. Always use `npx vercel` for deployment tasks.

---

## Deploying

When the user asks to deploy, publish, push changes, or share the prototype:

1. Run `npm run build` first to catch any build errors
2. Then run `npx vercel` from the project root
3. On first run: follow the prompts to link or create a Vercel project (a browser window will open for login)
4. Confirm the production URL once complete and share it with the user

---

## Subsequent deploys

After the first deploy, the project is linked to Vercel. All future deploys run:

```bash
npx vercel --prod
```

No prompts — goes straight to production.

---

## Checking deployment status

When the user asks if the project is deployed or live:
- Check whether a `.vercel` folder exists in the project root
- If yes: project is linked and has been deployed before
- If no: project has not been set up with Vercel yet

---

## Key rules

- Always run `npm run build` before deploying — fix any build errors first
- The entry point must be `app/page.tsx` (Next.js App Router convention)
- Environment variables (`.env.local`) are not deployed automatically — remind the user to add them in the Vercel dashboard under Project Settings → Environment Variables
- Never ask the user to open a terminal themselves — walk them through the commands step by step
